  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nMorning observations depict an expansive area of precipitation\nover much of the Mid-South, particularly over eastern Arkansas,\nthe Missouri Bootheel, and West Tennessee. Along and west of the\nMississippi River, precipitation takes on more of a stratiform\nappearance with forcing from the right entrance region of a 500 -\n200 mb jet streak and subtle low-level convergence due to an\nadvancing front. To the east of the Mississippi, convection is\ncurrently the predominant mode of precipitation as MUCAPE values\nsurge to between 500 - 1000 J/kg. Morning upper air data from LZK\nshows a very moist atmosphere containing 2.3\"+ PWATs. Therefore,\nshallow convection will be capable of heavy rainfall and\nlocalized flooding east of the Mississippi with light stratiform\nprecipitation elsewhere through tonight. Due to the large amount\nof cloud cover and widespread precipitation, highs are expected\nto remain in the 70s and 80s except for portions of northern\nMississippi where cloud cover will be minimal, leading to highs\nin the low 90s.\n\nA similar forecast is in the cards for tomorrow as the front\ncontinues to slowly sag south through Tonight. However, the\nmajority of the upper dynamics that will aid Friday's\nprecipitation will have left the region, leading to a lower\ncoverage of precipitation from the stratiform regime, which by\nthis point will exist over all but northern Mississippi. Northern\nMississippi on the other hand will still be able to generate\nMUCAPE values of at least 1000 J/kg, kicking off another round of\nthunderstorms through Saturday evening. Again, expect high\ntemperatures to remain close to the same as Friday in both space\nand magnitude. Additional showers are possible Sunday over\nnorthern Mississippi as a disturbance develops within the Gulf\nand pushes more moisture inland. Again, expect heavy downpours to\nbe the primary impact from any precipitation.\n\nInto Monday, the front will have made its way into central\nMississippi, erasing mentionable PoPs along with it. In its wake,\ncooler and drier weather will filter into the region with\nafternoon highs Monday and Tuesday in the middle to upper 70s.\nFurthermore, lows are forecast to drop into the 50s and even into\nthe upper 40s across portions of West Tennessee starting Monday.\nSimilar forecasts are expected through Wednesday with a gradual\nwarming trend through the end of the week as post-frontal\nadvection wanes.",
